{"step_index":1381,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T07:55:44Z","content":"**Hairpin NAT** (also known as **NAT Loopback** or **NAT Reflection**) is a network routing mechanism that allows a computer on an internal network to access a local server using that server’s **public IP address (or public domain name)**.\n\nIt gets its name from the sharp **180° U-turn** (like a hairpin or bobby pin) that the network packet makes inside the firewall router.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Real-Life Problem (Why you experienced that timeout)\n\nLet's use the exact IP addresses from your setup:\n\n* **Your Workstation (LAN):** `10.6.201.54`\n* **Centreon Reverse Proxy (DMZ):** `172.30.198.33`\n* **Firewall External VIP (Public IP):** `109.62.64.18`\n* **Domain Name:** `map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr`\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Client[\"Client PC\\n(10.6.201.54)\"]\n    FW[\"Firewall Router\\nPublic: 109.62.64.18\\nLAN: 10.6.0.1\"]\n    Server[\"Reverse Proxy\\n(172.30.198.33)\"]\n\n    Client -->|\"1. Target: 109.62.64.18\"| FW\n    FW -->|\"2. U-Turn (Hairpin)\"| Server\n    Server -->|\"3. Reply\"| FW\n    FW -->|\"4. Return to Client\"| Client\n```\n\nWhen you type `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443`:\n1. Your PC queries public DNS $\\rightarrow$ resolves to **`109.62.64.18`** (the firewall's public address).\n2. Your PC sends a packet out towards `109.62.64.18`.\n3. Even though the server is sitting in the very same datacenter, your packet travels to the firewall gateway.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What happens WITHOUT Hairpin NAT? (The Failure)\n\nBy default, corporate firewalls only apply Port Forwarding (DNAT) to traffic coming from the **outside (WAN interface)**.\n\nWhen a packet arrives from the **inside (LAN interface)** targeting the firewall's own public WAN address:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Client as Client (10.6.201.54)\n    participant FW as Firewall (109.62.64.18)\n    participant Server as Server (172.30.198.33)\n\n    Client->>FW: SYN [SRC: 10.6.201.54 -> DST: 109.62.64.18]\n    Note over FW: \"Wait, this came from LAN targeting my WAN IP!\"\n    FW--xClient: DROP (Packet discarded / Connection Timed Out)\n```\n\nEven if the firewall translates the destination (DNAT) to `172.30.198.33` without hairpinning, **Asymmetric Routing** causes a total crash:\n1. Server receives: `SRC: 10.6.201.54` $\\rightarrow$ `DST: 172.30.198.33`.\n2. Server notices `10.6.201.54` is an internal route, so it replies **directly** to the PC across internal switches: `SRC: 172.30.198.33` $\\rightarrow$ `DST: 10.6.201.54`.\n3. **The PC rejects the packet!** The PC thinks: *\"I asked `109.62.64.18` for a connection, who is this random `172.30.198.33` sending me SYN-ACK packets?!\"* $\\rightarrow$ The PC sends a TCP RST and kills the connection.\n\n---\n\n### 3. How Hairpin NAT Fixes This (Dual NAT: DNAT + SNAT)\n\nHairpin NAT solves this with a **double translation** performed right at the firewall's internal interface:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Client as Client (10.6.201.54)\n    participant FW as Firewall Router\n    participant Server as Server (172.30.198.33)\n\n    Client->>FW: 1. SYN [SRC: 10.6.201.54 -> DST: 109.62.64.18]\n    Note over FW: DNAT: DST -> 172.30.198.33<br/>SNAT: SRC -> Firewall LAN IP (e.g. 10.6.0.1)\n    FW->>Server: 2. SYN [SRC: 10.6.0.1 -> DST: 172.30.198.33]\n    Server->>FW: 3. SYN-ACK [SRC: 172.30.198.33 -> DST: 10.6.0.1]\n    Note over FW: Reverse NAT:<br/>SRC -> 109.62.64.18<br/>DST -> 10.6.201.54\n    FW->>Client: 4. SYN-ACK [SRC: 109.62.64.18 -> DST: 10.6.201.54]\n    Note over Client: Match! Connection Established (200 OK)\n```\n\n1. **DNAT (Destination NAT):** Rewrites destination `109.62.64.18` $\\rightarrow$ `172.30.198.33`.\n2. **SNAT (Source NAT):** Rewrites source `10.6.201.54` $\\rightarrow$ `Firewall's own internal IP`.\n   * *Why?* Because this forces the server to send the response back to the firewall instead of replying directly to the PC!\n3. When the firewall receives the server's reply, it un-NATs both addresses and returns the packet to the PC with `SRC: 109.62.64.18`.\n4. The client PC is happy and the TCP handshake succeeds.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Hairpin NAT vs. Split-Horizon DNS: Which is better?\n\n| Feature | Hairpin NAT | Split-Horizon DNS |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **How it works** | Firewall loops packets back via double NAT | Internal DNS gives internal IP directly to LAN users |\n| **Firewall Load** | Higher (Firewall processes all internal web traffic) | Zero (Traffic flows directly LAN $\\rightarrow$ DMZ) |\n| **Real Client IP** | Masked (Server logs see Firewall IP unless X-Forwarded-For is used) | Preserved (Server logs see client's real internal IP) |\n| **Configuration** | Configured on Firewall / Router rules | Configured on Corporate DNS Server (Active Directory / BIND) |\n| **Enterprise Standard** | Common in SOHO / Small business | **Industry Standard for Enterprises & Datacenters** |\n\nIn enterprise networks like yours, **Split-Horizon DNS** is almost always preferred over Hairpin NAT because it eliminates unnecessary load on the perimeter firewall and keeps internal traffic on local 10G/40G switching backbones.","thinking":"**Defining Hairpin NAT Concept**\n\n\"Hairpin\" NAT describes a network configuration where a device on a private network can access a service hosted on the *same* private network using the public IP address of the router. This works because the router \"hairpins\" the traffic, redirecting the external connection back internally. Instead of using the internal IP, the device uses the public IP and the router forwards the traffic back.\n\nHairpin NAT is essential in scenarios where devices need consistent addressing, regardless of their location (internal or external to the network). This setup is commonly needed when using port forwarding rules on a router or firewall, enabling external access to internal services, but also allowing internal devices to use the same public address to access the internal service.\n\n\n**Analyzing Internal Network Access**\n\nThe issue arises when an internal host attempts to reach an internal server using the external public IP, resolved from a public DNS record. Without \"hairpin\" NAT, the standard port forwarding (DNAT) rules are not applied because the packet originates from the LAN interface, leading to asymmetric routing. The server replies directly to the client with its internal IP, which the client rejects.\n\n\n"}
